.vscode/**
.claude/**
.gitignore
.sandbox/**
# Synchrony running on its own repository writes a real .synchrony here: this
# folder's plans, transcripts, logs and schedule. `.synchrony/.gitignore` keeps
# it out of git, but vsce reads this file and nothing else, so without this
# line every install ships one developer's private work.
.synchrony/**
# The same folder under its pre-rename name. An older build left installed
# beside this one kept a live `.chronos/` here, and the line above did not
# cover it: the 0.9.1 package went out with 78 run transcripts inside. Listed
# by name rather than as `.*/**` so it stays greppable.
.chronos/**
favicon.ico
# The raw manager and Tasks captures. Nothing in the extension loads them, and
# they are the unblurred originals: a real working-directory path and a real
# seven-day spend figure. They were half the size of the package. The blurred
# copies the landing page publishes live in `site/img/`, which is excluded below.
media/screenshots.png
media/screenshots-2.png
# A Windows folder-icon file this machine's shell writes into the repo root. It
# is not part of the extension and it names a real local path, so it must never
# ship. It stays on disk because deleting it just loses the folder's icon.
desktop.ini
# Previously built packages, if any are sitting in the root at package time.
*.vsix
# The three README screenshots. `repository` in package.json makes vsce rewrite
# the README's relative image links to github.com/.../raw/HEAD/media/... at
# package time, so both the Marketplace listing and the editor's extension page
# fetch these from GitHub and never touch the packaged copies. They were half
# the package. Keeping them tracked in git is what makes the README work.
media/Screenshot_1.png
media/Screenshot_2.png
media/Screenshot_3.png
# The landing page is repo-only — a static site Cloudflare Pages serves from
# GitHub. It must never ship inside the .vsix.
site/**
docs/**
# The instance dashboard is developer tooling, started by hand with
# `npm run dashboard`. Nothing in the extension loads either folder — the
# extension's only half of it is `dist/extension.js` writing the heartbeat.
dashboard/**
scripts/**
# The hub is the same kind of thing: a machine-wide HTTP MCP server the owner
# starts from a clone with `npm run hub` (see docs/HUB.md). Nothing in the
# installed extension spawns it, and its bundle was half the package.
dist/hub.js
# The Expo phone app is a separate project built and run on its own; its
# node_modules alone would balloon the vsix.
mobile/**
test/**
src/**
dist-test/**
node_modules/**
esbuild.js
reinstall.js
tsconfig.json
tsconfig.test.json
**/*.map
**/*.ts
